 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>Marni doesn’t need another reinvention. At least that’s what Meryll Rogge believes. For her second collection at the house, she’s doing something harder: figuring out what to keep. Her Marni is not a tabula rasa but a process of editing, pulling familiar codes into the present, stretching them, shrinking them, turning them inside out and occasionally giving them a good modernist shake.</p><p>“We definitely wanted to have a sense of continuity,” she said. “We want to establish the language, and we’ve also been thinking a lot about what to add to the language, what to take away.” Marni’s appeal has always been slightly awkward, occasionally strident, and defiantly un-Mediterranean: averse to overt sex-appeal, devoid of any prettiness; artistic, intellectual but un-cerebral. Rogge’s version retains that quirkiness while making it more dry, tinged with industrial, urban overtones. </p><p>Her starting point was Marni’s openness and instinct for contradiction. A latent ’60s influence surfaced almost unconsciously, Rogge said, while the broader historical conversation stretched across the late ’90s and early 2000s. Marni’s archive is less a timeline than a box of things worth stealing from: softness seeps into structure, sportswear unsettles tailoring, useful details corrode decoration. Sack dresses in raw calfskin turned the sweetness of their silhouettes into something tougher, almost brutalist in tone, offset by modernistic embroidery. A bejeweled appliqué was lifted from one garment and placed on another, closer to an objet trouvé than an ornament. And ornament itself suggested a deliberately excessive gesture: “It’s like a decoration on the decoration,” said Rogge.</p><p>Scale was another way of unsettling the codes. Buckles became enormous, then mini, mini, mini. Classic Marni elastic was enlarged; tiny buttons were blown up and displaced onto leather and wool. An abstract print took the idea of a ’60s/’70s dress and reduced it to an almost unrecognizable graphic. Archive references were treated as raw material. The same goes for construction. Openings, exposed linings and fragmented surfaces made the inside visible; a brassière outline was built into a garment, turning an underlayer outward. The industrial triple stitch became a visible signature. Hardware migrated between clothes and accessories. Color carried a human reference: grays, brunettes, blacks and blondes borrowed from hair colors, suggesting different stages of life without turning the idea into a demographic spreadsheet. Marni’s old appetite for the slightly wrong was still there, only it was given a sharper set of tools. “It’s sexy, but absolutely not sexy,” Rogge said, which may be the most Marni sentence imaginable. There was sensuality in the exposure, but no attempt to make the clothes conventionally seductive. Seduction was definitely not the point.</p><p>For men, workwear and tailoring intersected in suit trousers with utilitarian details; color-blocking and knits were striped with tiny sequins. Halter necks and other unconventional openings loosened the conventional male silhouette. Said Rogge: “It’s not flamboyant; it’s just discreet, but present.” </p><p>Even the show set followed the logic of accumulation and subtraction. Working with Formafantasma, Rogge kept exactly the same layout and paneling as last season, then stripped away the layers underneath. “It’s like our way to say we’re evolving and we’re looking towards the future,” she said. Materials from the previous set were pulped and transformed into handmade paper, which became paper airplanes for the invitations, a charmingly literal metaphor for moving forward without having to burn down the runway first.</p><p>Rogge said she puts “a lot of myself” into Marni, but the collection was equally conscious of what belongs to the house. Her task is not to replace its language but to sharpen it: to preserve the instinctive oddness, the artiness, the friction, while giving it a more deliberate grammar. “We want to make sure that we bring our best Marni forward,” she said. Perhaps, in Marni’s case, the best version is the one that remains oddly elusive, and slightly difficult to pin down.</p></div>",
